<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/intermediate/gender-and-society">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Gender sociology studies <strong>how societies script masculinity and femininity — and what
    the scripts cost and enable</strong>. Its method: notice the taken-for-granted (who does the
    dishes, who speaks first, whose job title sounds impressive) and trace it to structure
    rather than nature.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three layers, the structure, the care economy, the stall" widget="equationStep" config={genderCfg} />

  <h2>Evidence the field returns to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The pay gap decomposes honestly:</strong> raw gaps (~18% in the US) shrink when occupation, hours and experience are controlled (~5–8% unexplained) — the debate is what the residual means (discrimination? the penalty for care years?) and both findings matter.</li>
    <li><strong>Cross-cultural variation is the argument:</strong> what counts as men's work varies by society and era — variation is the natural experiment showing construction at work.</li>
    <li><strong>Gender and language:</strong> the linguistics ladder shows grammatical gender, generic pronouns, and how speech patterns get gendered and policed — language is one of gender's main enforcement channels.</li>
    <li><strong>Masculinity studies emerged:</strong> Connell's "hegemonic masculinity" — hierarchy among men, not just between men and women; male suicide rates and educational underachievement entered the data. The field widened.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> West & Zimmerman, "Doing Gender" (1987); Hochschild, The Second Shift (1989); Ridgeway, Framed by Gender (2011); Connell (1995).</p>
</TopicPage>
